      "latency_ms": 1907.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 792,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat without large temperature change, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires much energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, enabling chemical reactions in biological systems.<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between molecules creates a “skin” on the surface, allowing insects to walk on water and plants to transport water upward (capillary action).<br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating aquatic life in cold climates. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 361 million km²). Found in oceans, rivers, lakes, glaciers, groundwater, and the atmosphere. Also present in living organisms (≈ 60 % of the human body by weight). |\n| **Biological role** | • **Medium for biochemical reactions** – enzymes, metabolism, DNA replication, etc., all occur in aqueous environments.<br>• **Transport** – blood, sap, and cellular fluids move nutrients, waste, and signals.<br>• **Regulation** – maintains temperature (through sweating, respiration) and pH balance. |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | Drinking, agriculture, sanitation, power generation (hydropower, steam turbines), manufacturing (chemical synthesis, cooling), recreation (swimming, sports), and many more. |\n| **Environmental importance** | • **Hydrologic cycle** – evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff continuously redistribute water, shaping weather and ecosystems.<br>• **Climate regulation** – oceans store and transport heat around the globe.<br>• **Habitat** – provides the environment for countless aquatic species. |\n\n### Why water is unique\n- **Polarity + hydrogen bonding:** Each water molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds with neighbors, creating a dynamic, cohesive network that gives water its unusual thermal and mechanical properties.\n- **Broad liquid range:** Unlike many small molecules, water remains liquid over a wide temperature range (0 °C–100 °C at 1 atm), which is ideal for supporting life.\n- **Self‑ionization:** A tiny fraction of water molecules dissociate into H⁺ and OH⁻ ions (pKw ≈ 14 at 25 °C), providing a natural buffer system for acid–base chemistry.\n\n### In a nutshell\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) whose ability to form extensive hydrogen bonds gives it remarkable physical and chemical properties. These properties make it an unparalleled solvent, a regulator of temperature, a participant in the planet’s water cycle, and the medium in which virtually all known biological processes occur."
